WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.500 --> 00:00:06.454
Orleans.Lattice, in three minutes: why it
exists, what it is, and where to start.

2
00:00:07.454 --> 00:00:11.450
Almost every application has
to remember things: what is in a basket,

3
00:00:11.450 --> 00:00:14.461
who may open a document,
how often a page is viewed.

4
00:00:14.811 --> 00:00:16.654
Developers call that memory state.

5
00:00:17.654 --> 00:00:20.510
Many applications run
in more than one place at once,

6
00:00:20.510 --> 00:00:22.828
so that they keep going
if one place fails.

7
00:00:23.178 --> 00:00:27.089
Then two places can change the same thing
at the same moment.

8
00:00:27.439 --> 00:00:31.095
The usual answer is to make them
take turns, with a lock or a vote,

9
00:00:31.095 --> 00:00:34.915
and to keep the memory in a separate
database, beside the application.

10
00:00:35.265 --> 00:00:37.580
Orleans.Lattice takes
a different approach.

11
00:00:37.580 --> 00:00:40.325
It keeps the working memory
inside the application.

12
00:00:40.675 --> 00:00:44.750
And it records each change so
that every place reaches the same answer,

13
00:00:44.750 --> 00:00:46.873
in whatever order the changes arrive.

14
00:00:46.873 --> 00:00:48.193
Neither waits its turn.

15
00:00:48.543 --> 00:00:50.916
It runs on one machine
with no cloud account,

16
00:00:50.916 --> 00:00:53.236
and grows to many regions
without a rewrite.

17
00:00:54.236 --> 00:00:58.687
In technical terms, Orleans.Lattice
is a platform for building durable,

18
00:00:58.687 --> 00:01:01.634
distributed state systems
on Microsoft Orleans.

19
00:01:01.984 --> 00:01:06.724
At its centre is a sorted key-value store
that runs inside your own cluster,

20
00:01:06.724 --> 00:01:10.341
with no external database,
coordinator or queue beside it.

21
00:01:10.691 --> 00:01:12.273
It takes three positions.

22
00:01:14.473 --> 00:01:16.623
First: the store lives in the cluster.

23
00:01:16.623 --> 00:01:20.584
State is held by grains, in the same
cluster as the code that uses it.

24
00:01:20.934 --> 00:01:25.237
So a read is a grain call, not a round
trip to a separate database tier

25
00:01:25.237 --> 00:01:27.782
with its own scaling and its own failures.

26
00:01:28.782 --> 00:01:31.819
Second: conflict resolution is algebraic.

27
00:01:32.169 --> 00:01:34.668
Two clusters write at the same time.

28
00:01:34.668 --> 00:01:38.139
Cluster A adds three,
and cluster B, concurrently,

29
00:01:38.139 --> 00:01:40.429
adds five, each to its own count.

30
00:01:40.429 --> 00:01:42.720
Neither state is above the other.

31
00:01:43.070 --> 00:01:47.294
Each merges the other's update
by taking the larger value per replica,

32
00:01:47.294 --> 00:01:50.010
so both arrive at the same state:
their join.

33
00:01:50.360 --> 00:01:53.244
Deliver that update twice,
and nothing changes.

34
00:01:53.594 --> 00:01:57.655
Merges are commutative,
associative and idempotent.

35
00:01:58.005 --> 00:02:00.793
So any cluster can accept
a write to any key,

36
00:02:00.793 --> 00:02:03.829
with no lock manager
and no consensus round trip.

37
00:02:03.829 --> 00:02:06.245
For plain values, the last writer wins.

38
00:02:07.245 --> 00:02:09.319
Third: everything else is a seam.

39
00:02:09.319 --> 00:02:12.964
Storage, identity, governance,
replication, administration

40
00:02:12.964 --> 00:02:17.049
and observability are companion
packages behind documented seams.

41
00:02:17.399 --> 00:02:21.897
A host takes only what it registers, and
a capability it leaves out costs nothing.

42
00:02:22.897 --> 00:02:25.670
So a deployment can grow
without a rewrite.

43
00:02:25.670 --> 00:02:28.443
Start local: one machine,
no cloud account.

44
00:02:28.793 --> 00:02:32.826
Add a team: identity,
authorization, schemas and tenants.

45
00:02:33.176 --> 00:02:38.298
Go global: active in every region at once,
with backup and an autoscaling signal.

46
00:02:38.648 --> 00:02:41.750
At every stage,
the programming model is the same.

47
00:02:41.750 --> 00:02:44.355
Your code resolves ILattice, and calls it.

48
00:02:45.355 --> 00:02:47.841
The documentation has three ways in.

49
00:02:48.191 --> 00:02:51.551
Build,
if you are writing code against ILattice.

50
00:02:51.901 --> 00:02:54.956
Evaluate,
if you are deciding whether it fits.

51
00:02:55.306 --> 00:02:58.162
Operate, if you are running an estate.

52
00:02:58.162 --> 00:02:58.989
You choose.
